The compilation error `implicit declaration of function 'k_is_user_thread'` indicates that the function `k_is_user_thread` is not declared when the file `subsys/testsuite/ztest/src/ztest_error_hook.c` is compiled.

The function `k_is_user_thread` is defined in `<zephyr/kernel.h>` as an inline function, but its definition is guarded by `CONFIG_USERSPACE`. The build configuration that is failing likely has `CONFIG_SPIN_VALIDATE` enabled but `CONFIG_USERSPACE` disabled. In this case, `k_is_user_thread` is not available, leading to the compilation error.

The fix is to guard the usage of `k_is_user_thread` with an `#ifdef CONFIG_USERSPACE` block. When `CONFIG_USERSPACE` is not defined, there are no user threads, so the check is not needed. In that case, we can assume the thread is not a user thread and proceed.

The same problematic code pattern exists in two places in the file: once in `k_sys_fatal_error_handler` and once in `assert_post_action`. Both need to be fixed to ensure the code compiles correctly under all configurations.

